exports[`doc: the 18 sections > doc: snapshot > shape A 1`] = `
"# What pipegen set up in this repository

pipegen wrote this file in plain English. If you edit it, pipegen keeps your edits and never replaces the file.

## What your GitHub plan allows

This section comes first so that no limit is hidden.

- This repository is private. Your GitHub plan: team.
- Branch rules: available, so the check \`pipegen / gate\` can be required before a pull request merges.
- Environments: available. The environment \`pipegen-signing\` holds the signing key and limits the verify step to the default branch.
- Required reviewers on an environment: available.
- Deployment branch policies: available, so only the default branch can use the signing environment.
- Build attestations: available.
- Code scanning results: available.
- Keyless signing: allowed (a public repository: its signing log is public).
- Anyone who can add a workflow can add a job that reports under the name of the gate. The guard is CODEOWNERS plus a required code-owner review of the pipeline files. On a private repository with a free plan GitHub can not enforce that review, so this is a limit that stays.
- Where GitHub can not enforce this (a private repository on a free plan, or a repository with one writer), the deploy check trusts the people who can edit the workflow files.
- Pull requests from forks: on a public repository, a first-time contributor's workflow run waits for a maintainer to approve it. On a private repository, workflows on fork pull requests are off by default. A check that needs a secret shows as not run on a fork pull request.
- The network block on CI jobs lets only the allowed hosts through. A site that sits behind the same CDN as an allowed host stays reachable, so the block limits traffic and is not a wall.

### Locks on merging

- Only one person can write to "shop", so pull requests need no approval.

## What pipegen set up

- pipegen v0.2 is for repositories owned by CrisWorking. A public repository uses the gate in the normal way.
- This repository is private, so its checks run from central workflows in the pipelines repository of the same owner, pinned to one commit. The caller file in this repository is \`.github/workflows/pipegen.yml\`.
- pipegen does not change what or how you deploy. It adds a verify job, \`needs\` entries on your existing deploy jobs, and its head check as the first step of each of those jobs (so a deploy that waited for an approval, or a re-run, stops once a newer commit is on the default branch), and nothing else in them. Two kinds of deploy job get no head check: a job that calls another workflow (it has no steps of its own), and one pipegen gates only for its runner or vars that can not start with the check; the notes below say which jobs and what that leaves open.
- pipegen offers three strictness levels, Starter, Standard (the default) and Strict. They decide which checks block a pull request and which only warn.
- Estimated monthly Actions minutes: about 120, counted from the activity of the last 30 days. Your plan includes 3000 minutes a month for private repositories.

### Services pipegen found

- Service "shop-api" (node), type: server, container.
- Service "jobs-runner" (node), type: cli.

### Checks your repository already has

- The tests run on every pull request into main and every push to main.
- Pushes to branches without a pull request aren't checked.

## Fix a blocked pull request

The required check is \`pipegen / gate\`. When it fails, or only warns, its summary on the run gives messages. The first column holds the start of the message as the gate writes it (or the rule name of an integrity finding), so you can find your line.

| Message | What it means | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a new critical, high or secret finding blocks in any mode | A check found a serious problem or a secret that the base branch did not have. | Fix it in the pull request. A false alarm is for a code owner to judge; adding a suppression comment is flagged itself. Treat a secret as leaked and rotate it: a secret that was ever pushed stays in the history. |
| secret added in this PR's history | A scan of the commits of this pull request found a secret. A commit that added a secret still counts after a later commit took it out. Any finding in the commit range blocks. | Rotate the secret now. Then rewrite the branch so the commits no longer hold it, and push again. |
| the pull request's commits are not proven free of secrets | The scan of the commits of this pull request could not run or gave no result, so nothing is proven. It blocks on a pull request. | Run the job again. |
| a new vulnerability in dependency review | A package added by the pull request has a known vulnerability that the base did not have. | Pick another package or version. |
| a malicious package advisory (MAL-): blocks even when old | A dependency matches a malicious-package advisory. It blocks even when it is old. | Remove the package and rotate anything it could have read. |
| an unknown licence: needs a human, never blocks | A package has a licence the check does not know. It only warns. | Have a person check the licence. |
| integrity:protected-path | The pull request changes a workflow, a tool config or an ignore file (a file that is part of the pipeline), and no code owner approved this exact commit. An approval counts only for the commit it was given on, so a code owner who approved an older commit has to approve the latest one. | Ask a code owner to approve the latest commit. After a new push the approval has to be given again. |
| integrity:suppression | Added lines turn a check off for that code (a suppression comment). | Remove the suppression, or ask a code owner to approve this exact commit. |
| integrity:incomplete-list | The pull request is too large to check (3,000 or more files), or GitHub did not give the whole file list, so the gate can not see every change. | Split the pull request into smaller ones, or run the checks again if the branch moved. |
| integrity:no-patch | GitHub gave no readable change for that file, so the gate can not check it for suppressions. | Make the change smaller or split the file change into its own pull request. A code owner can approve the exact commit. |
| integrity:case-extension | A new file has a scanned language's extension in another letter case, so the scanners would skip it while a runtime may still load it. | Rename the file to the lower-case extension. |
| pipeline files changed or suppressions added without code-owner approval on the current commit | This heading of the summary comes with the integrity findings above. The pull request is blocked until a code owner approves the current commit. | Ask a code owner to approve the latest commit. |
| installing pipegen: review these files | This is the pull request that installs pipegen, so the pipeline files are new. It warns and does not block. | Read the files, then merge. |
| tool error in | A check could not finish (it crashed, ran out of time or its result could not be read). A required check that errors blocks; a not proven check only warns. | Read the check's log, fix the cause and run the job again. |
| did not report | A scanner finished without a result. A required one blocks; a not proven or non-blocking one only warns or notes it. | Run the job again. |
| not run on a fork PR (no secrets) | GitHub gives no secrets to a pull request from a fork, so a check that needs one did not run. If the check is required, the pull request is blocked. | A maintainer can push the branch into this repository and open the pull request from there. |
| not-proven check | pipegen could not prove the check on your repository yet (see Not proven yet). It warns and does not block. | Set the secret names listed under Not proven yet. The nightly run then opens an issue and \`npx pipegen update\` applies the change. |
| non-blocking check | A check that never blocks. The summary notes its result. | Read the note; nothing is required. |
| warn-first check | A check judged by its job status, in its own warn period: a failed job is a warning until a reviewed pull request makes the check blocking. | Fix the failure before the period ends (see Warn mode). |
| warn-first: a warning until a reviewed pull request makes the check blocking | A finding of a check that reads findings, in its own warn period, is a warning. A new critical, high or secret finding still blocks, and so does a failed job of such a check (an image scan or a Dockerfile lint, for example). | Fix the findings before the period ends (see Warn mode). |
| not proven: never blocks | A finding of a check that is not proven yet is a warning and never blocks. | Make the check proven (see Not proven yet). |
| a non-blocking check: never blocks | A finding of a check that is not meant to block is shown and never blocks. | Read it; nothing is required. |
| required check | A required check failed, so the pull request is blocked. | Open the job's log, fix the failure and push again. |
| is not in the job list (missing) | The gate expects a check that did not run, so the pull request can not merge. | Run the workflow again. With the org required workflow, re-run the \`pipegen required\` run after a code owner approves. |
| the job list is empty | No check ran, so nothing is proven and the gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| result tampered or missing | The result file of a check is missing, or its checksum differs from the one its job reported. The gate does not trust it and blocks. | Run the checks again. If it keeps happening, someone changed the pipeline: run \`npx pipegen verify\`. |
| no coverage for | A check ran but could not look (for example a scan that reported nothing because it had nothing to read). A required one blocks, because nothing was proven. | Fix what the check could not read, then run the checks again. |
| no base: | The base scan could not be used for this event, so every finding compared with it counts as new. A new critical or high finding therefore blocks, and so does a new secret. Only a scanner that could not run on the base commit of the pull request that installs pipegen warns instead (the next row). | Run the checks again. On a new install, merge the setup pull request and the next pull request has a base. |
| no base: the scanner couldn't run on the base commit (installing pipegen) | Image findings have no earlier scan to compare with yet: on the pull request that installs pipegen they warn, and on every other pull request they block. | Fix the finding in the image or its base image. |
| input is refused | One of the gate's own inputs (the job list, the policy, the service roots, the protected paths, a date) is not one the gate accepts. The gate blocks, because it can not trust its own settings. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| input is missing | One of the gate's own inputs (the job list, the GitHub token, the service roots) is missing. The gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| the jobs input (toJSON(needs)) is | The results of the jobs the gate reads are missing, too large or not an object. The gate blocks. | Run the workflow again. If it keeps happening, run \`npx pipegen verify\`. |
| the mode input is not gate | The gate was started in a mode other than gate. The gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| does not take the input | The gate was given an input it does not know, or the wrong mode. The gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| the policy is refused | The gate's own settings are wrong, so it uses the default policy and blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| not-proven can't include floor need | A check every repository needs is listed as not proven, which the gate does not allow. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| can never be skipped | A check that can never be skipped is listed as skipped. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| warn-first can't be set on floor need | A check every repository needs was put in warn-first mode, which the gate does not allow. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| skipped, | You skipped that part with --skip, so it has no job and gives no protection. The summary lists it under Skipped. | Run pipegen again without the skip if you want the part. |
| (warn mode) | The summary heading says warn mode: old findings and medium ones warn, and a new critical, high or secret finding still blocks. | Fix the warnings before warn mode ends (see Warn mode). |
| (block mode) | The summary heading says block mode: the warn period is over, so what used to warn now blocks. | Fix the finding. Nothing else changed. |

## The four layers

Each check has a tool, a reason and a mode. The mode is worked out from what pipegen wrote and from the gate's own rules, not only from what the plan asked for.

### Pre-commit layer

Runs on your machine before each commit.

- Secret scan, tool betterleaks/betterleaks. Looks for passwords, keys and tokens in the code and in the commits of a pull request. Blocks the commit.
- Format and lint, service "shop-api", tool biomejs/biome. Checks formatting and common mistakes on the files you commit. Not set up: the format and lint hook was left out (it needs the repository's own Biome at the reviewed version in its lockfile; see the notes below).

### Push layer

Runs on a push to a branch that has no open pull request.

- Secret scan, tool betterleaks/betterleaks. Looks for passwords, keys and tokens in the code and in the commits of a pull request. Fails the run.

### Merge layer

Runs on every pull request and decides whether it can merge. The gate (the check \`pipegen / gate\`) reads every result.

A check marked "Blocks a pull request" blocks only after you run \`npx pipegen finish\`, which turns on the merge locks. Until then a failed gate shows on the pull request but does not stop the merge.

- Secret scan, tool betterleaks/betterleaks. Looks for passwords, keys and tokens in the code and in the commits of a pull request. Blocks a pull request.
- Code scan, service "shop-api", tool semgrep/semgrep. Looks for insecure code patterns in your source. Blocks a pull request.
- Code scan, service "jobs-runner", tool semgrep/semgrep. Looks for insecure code patterns in your source. Blocks a pull request.
- Dependency scan, service "shop-api", tool google/osv-scanner. Looks for known vulnerabilities in the packages you depend on. Blocks a pull request.
- Dependency scan, service "jobs-runner", tool google/osv-scanner. Looks for known vulnerabilities in the packages you depend on. Blocks a pull request.
- The gate, tool the pipegen gate action. Reads every result and decides whether the pull request can merge. Blocks a pull request.
- Workflow lint, tool rhysd/actionlint. Checks GitHub workflow files for unsafe patterns. Blocks a pull request.
- Workflow pin check, tool suzuki-shunsuke/pinact. Checks that the actions in workflows are pinned to a commit. Blocks a pull request.
- Unit tests, service "shop-api", no tool listed (see the notes below). Runs your tests so a change that breaks them is caught. Blocks a pull request.
- Type check, service "shop-api", tool python/mypy. Runs the type checker. Blocks a pull request.
- Test coverage, service "shop-api", tool vitest-dev/vitest. Checks how much of the code the tests run. Starts in warn mode: a failure warns until a reviewed pull request makes the check blocking.
- Copy-paste check, tool kucherenko/jscpd. Finds code copied from other places in the repository. Runs for information; the gate does not use it.
- Image build, service "shop-api", tool docker/build-push-action. Builds the container image of the pull request without publishing it. Blocks a pull request.
- Image scan, service "shop-api", tool aquasecurity/trivy. Scans the built image for known vulnerabilities. Blocks a pull request.
- Running app scan, service "shop-api", tool zaproxy/zaproxy. Starts the app and probes it the way an attacker would. Warns only: it is not proven yet (see Not proven yet).
- Licence check, tool aquasecurity/trivy. Checks the licences of your dependencies. Blocks a pull request.

### Deploy layer

Runs on a push to the default branch: it builds, signs and checks the images that are deployed.

- Publishing, service "shop-api", no tool listed (see the notes below). Publishes a package with a short-lived token instead of a stored one. Fails the run.
- Image signing, service "shop-api", no tool listed (see the notes below). Signs the published image so a deploy can check where it came from. Not set up: pipegen publishes no image of this service, so it signs and verifies nothing for it (see Signing and the notes below).

### Limits of these checks

- The type-check follows your own tsconfig.json. The repo's tsconfig decides what tsc checks and how strictly, and a pull request can edit it (\`exclude\`, \`skipLibCheck\`, \`extends\`, a \`ts-nocheck\` comment). pipegen refuses only the plain no-op layouts: no file selected, \`noCheck\`, and a missing or linked tsconfig.json. It is not a protected path.
- The raw result of the running app scan travels unscrubbed between the job that runs the app and the job that reads the result. It holds only what an app without secrets answered. A hostile app can always answer a scanner cleanly.
- The running app scan has no earlier scan to compare with, so its findings warn on the pull request that installs pipegen and count as new after it.
- The running app scan talks to ZAP through its API, which answers only 127.0.0.1, so the scanned app can not reach it.
- Image findings have no base yet: there is no earlier scan of the base commit to compare with, so image findings warn on the pull request that installs pipegen and block on every other.
- The licence scan reads lockfiles only.
- The code scan runs Semgrep with a pinned copy of the semgrep-rules repository. Those rules have their own licence (the Semgrep Rules License v1.0). It lets you use them to check your own code, but not to sell them or offer them as a service. Read it before you use the rules anywhere else.
- Publishing uses a trusted publisher, so no token is stored. Set it up once: on npmjs.com open the package settings and add a trusted publisher for GitHub Actions with this repository and the publish workflow file name; on PyPI open the project, then Publishing, and add a trusted publisher with the same details. The publish job needs npm 11.5.1 or newer and Node 22.14.0 or newer, and it is the only job that gets the \`id-token\` permission.
- The copy-paste check warns only, at first: it reports new copy-paste in changed code, and old copies are not counted. A repository can make it blocking. The mode of a check is part of the pipeline files, which are protected paths, so that change goes through a pull request that a code owner approves.
- Each service installs with \`npm ci\`, which needs a \`package-lock.json\` in that service's folder. A lock file at the workspace root is not enough: the check then fails closed, it does not pass.
- Project scripts that pipegen runs get no declared secrets yet. A test that needs a secret can get it only through the coverage check.
- npm only: pnpm, yarn and bun are not supported yet. A Vue, Svelte or Astro TypeScript repository stops at assembly with a plain reason, because pipegen has no reviewed type checker for it.
- The nightly job has a \`concurrency\` group, so runs never overlap. An issue it opened and any bot closed (even a stale bot running as the github-actions bot) is reopened by design. Only a person can dismiss it.
- After rewriting main's history (a force-push), the gate may stay in block mode until 10 green pushes to main have followed.

### Nightly scans

- Every night a scan reads the whole git history (every branch and tag) for secrets and reports what it finds in code scanning. It never blocks a pull request: a secret it finds has already leaked, so rotate it.
- A permanent limit of the history scan: a merge commit with three or more parents (an octopus merge) anywhere in the history makes the nightly history job fail every night, because its own changes can not be shown. Only a history rewrite removes it.
- Another permanent limit: a commit dated after 1 January 2100 on any branch or tag makes the same job fail every night, because the scan proves it read the whole history with marker commits of that date.
- Every night the running app scan runs again with nuclei and with ZAP's active scan, which sends attack requests to the app the nightly run starts from its own image build. The results go to code scanning and never block a pull request.

### Notes from the build

- A note from the build.

## Suggestions (not running)

A suggestion is a tool that pipegen does not run for you. It may be a tool pipegen has not reviewed, or a reviewed tool that had nothing to run on in this repository (its reason says which). Suggestions are mention-only: they never run. A tool can join the reviewed list only through a reviewed pull request to pipegen.

- "trufflehog" (repository trufflesecurity/trufflehog): It also checks the commit history for keys.

## Files pipegen created and why

- \`PIPEGEN.md\`: this file.
- \`.github/workflows/pipegen.yml\`: the workflow that starts the pipeline.
- In the pipelines repository: the central workflows \`shop--push.yml\`, \`shop--merge.yml\`, \`shop--nightly.yml\` and the shared signing workflow, with the install record.
- Dependabot, in \`.github/dependabot.yml\` when pipegen adds an entry: it keeps the pinned actions up to date, each update at least 7 days old. The entry ignores the central repository, because Dependabot can not read it, so Dependabot does not bump the central commit. pipegen keeps that pin fresh: \`npx pipegen update\` opens the pull request and the nightly run warns when the pin is old. There are two opt-ins, not on by default: an organisation grant through the API, or a git registry entry with a read-only token you make yourself. A repository that uses Renovate keeps it and gets no Dependabot file (set \`minimumReleaseAge\` to 7 days in Renovate).
- "lefthook.yml": t
- Files that were already in your repository, identical to what pipegen writes, are listed in the setup pull request. pipegen never rewrites them, and uninstall asks before it deletes one.

## Your tests

Starter tests describe what your code does today. They are a coverage floor, not proof: they can lock in today's bugs, so review them. App code runs with full power (process, network and files), so the CI job that runs it has no secrets, a read-only token and no network except the registry.

If a coverage waiver is needed, the owners in pipegen's CODEOWNERS block can approve it, also for a file whose own CODEOWNERS rule names no user.

- Service "shop-api": pipegen keeps your tests. This service already has tests, so pipegen keeps them.

## Skipped parts

- Part slack: skipped with --skip. No Slack here. What is missing: There are no Slack messages about findings.

## Signing

Images are signed with a cosign key. \`npx pipegen finish\` makes the key pair and stores it as environment secrets of \`pipegen-signing\`, which only the default branch can use. The public key is the environment variable \`PIPEGEN_COSIGN_PUB\` of \`pipegen-signing\`. The verify job checks the sha256 of that variable against the value in the install record before it uses it, so a changed variable is a finding. Signing uses \`--tlog-upload=false\`.

\`npx pipegen finish --rotate-key\` makes a new key pair. Images signed with the old key no longer verify. The verify literal changes through a pull request, and verify fails closed until that pull request is merged. A key rotation resets the gate's marker and baseline, and \`npx pipegen verify\` says so.

Signing covers images only. A service that builds no image has report-only signing: pipegen signs nothing for it, and the notes in The four layers name each one.

## Deploys

### Unprotected deploys

- ".github/workflows/release.yml" (triggers: workflow_dispatch): It starts by hand.
- A deploy workflow with any other trigger than a push to the default branch gets no verify job, so it is an unprotected gap. A deploy started by \`workflow_run\` is a gap too, and so is a deploy from a branch other than the default branch (the verify job passes only for the default branch).
- Each edit pipegen made to a deploy workflow is listed under Not proven yet until you have checked it.

## Not proven yet

- Part pipeline, Running app scan: The scan needs a login that pipegen was not given. Secret names to set: \`APP_LOGIN_TOKEN\`.

Set a missing secret in the repository settings: Settings, Secrets and variables, Actions ([the secrets page of this repository](https://github.com/acme/shop/settings/secrets/actions)). When the key exists, the nightly run opens an issue with the exact patch: run \`npx pipegen update\` and it applies the change as a pull request. CI never edits workflow files itself.

The not-proven list is one line in \`.github/workflows/shop--merge.yml\` in the pipelines repository, marked \`pipegen:not-proven\`. It never holds a check that every repository needs.

## Pipeline integrity

- The gate protects the files that define the pipeline: workflows, tool configs, ignore files and the hook files. A pull request that changes one is blocked unless a code owner approved the exact head commit.
- Code owners in pipegen's CODEOWNERS block: "alice", "bob". 3 people can write to this repository.
- A one-person repository: pull requests need no approval, so whoever merges can also change the pipeline that judges them, and pipegen can not prevent that. Run \`npx pipegen verify\` to see whether the pipeline still matches what pipegen set up.
- Code-owner review is the guard against a job that fakes the gate's name. Where GitHub can not enforce it, the limit is stated in the first section.
- A patch issue the nightly run opens is only a prompt: \`npx pipegen update\` works out the change itself from its built-in reviewed pins.

## Warn mode

Warn mode lasts until 7 days after you merge (the exact date is set by \`npx pipegen finish\`).

In warn mode old findings and medium ones warn, and a new critical, high or secret finding still blocks.

There are two timers. The first (7 days after you merge) is for the whole gate: it warns until then and blocks after. The second is for one check: a new blocking check can start in its own warn mode for about 14 days, and then the nightly run opens an issue to switch it to blocking.

These checks start in warn mode:

- Test coverage

## Deploying old commits and retention

Runs past the retention period can not be verified, so re-run the pipeline first before you deploy an old commit. GitHub Actions keeps runs and artifacts 90 days by default. On private repositories you can raise it to 400 days (Settings, Actions, General).

## The debate

pipegen chose its tools in a short debate between AI models. The code, not the AI, checks every choice against pipegen's reviewed list.

### Scoreboard

- The builder won the sast check, 2 to 1.

### Transcript

- Builder: use semgrep for sast.

## Hooks setup

After you clone the repository, run \`lefthook install\` once, so the hooks pipegen wrote in \`lefthook.yml\` are active on your machine.

The secrets hook runs pipegen from npm, at the exact version that wrote the hook, and pipegen runs its pinned secret scanner on the staged changes. The command is:

\`\`\`sh
npx --yes --prefix=/ --registry=https://registry.npmjs.org pipegen@VERSION hook secrets
\`\`\`

VERSION is the exact pipegen version that wrote the hook. The first commit trusts npm's metadata for that exact version. It is not pinned by a lockfile hash.

- The hook needs \`node\` and \`npx\` and a connection to the npm registry, in every language repository. If one is missing the hook fails and blocks the commit (it fails closed).
- \`git commit --no-verify\` is the way out when npm is unreachable or the version is not published. CI still scans.
- If the pipegen version that wrote the hook is not on npm (a build that was never published), every commit fails with npm's own \`E404\` text. That means npm has no such version. Use \`git commit --no-verify\` until a published version is in the hook.
- In CI the hooks run with \`--all-files\`, so nothing is staged and the secrets hook passes at once. The secret scan job of CI is the cover.
- The local hook is a convenience a repo's own scripts can turn off; CI is the gate.

## Run again, finish, verify, update and uninstall

- Run the pipegen setup again at any time. It updates the same pull request \`pipegen/setup\` and opens central changes through a central pull request, so there are no duplicates.
- A re-run with \`--yes\`, or in CI, needs the local record file. Without it pipegen shows what the install record claims and you type \`owner/repo\` to confirm.
- \`npx pipegen finish\` runs after you merge the setup pull request. It turns on the merge locks and the settings pipegen planned. None of them is turned on before the merge, or the setup pull request could not merge.
- \`npx pipegen finish --print\` changes nothing: it prints the exact commands, so you can run them yourself.
- \`npx pipegen finish --rotate-key\` makes a new signing key pair (key mode only). Old images do not verify with the new key, and the verify literal changes through a pull request.
- \`npx pipegen verify\` compares the pipeline in the repository with the install record. It also warns about a CODEOWNERS rule after pipegen's block that names only teams: the gate can not read a team's members, so name users there.
- \`npx pipegen update\` makes only gate pin swaps and the central re-pin. It never touches \`PIPEGEN.md\`. A file you edited is never overwritten; it goes to a conflict list.
- Uninstall removes only what the install record shows pipegen made. It asks before it deletes a file that was already in your repository.

## Words used

- Gate: The check \`pipegen / gate\`. It reads every result and decides whether a pull request can merge.
- Finding: One problem a check reports, such as a vulnerable package or a leaked key.
- Baseline: The findings that were already there before a pull request, so only new ones count against it.
- Warn mode: A period in which most findings warn and do not block.
- Protected path: A file that defines the pipeline. Changing it needs a code-owner approval.
- Code owner: A person named in CODEOWNERS whose review is required for the files they own.
- Ruleset: GitHub's rules for a branch, such as a required check or a required review.
- Not proven: A check pipegen could not prove on your repository yet. It warns and does not block.
- Verify-first: A job that runs before a deploy and checks the commit passed the gate and the image is the signed one.
- Signing: A signature on an image that shows which workflow made it.
- Digest: The checksum that names one exact image. A digest never changes; a tag can move.
- Suggestion: A tool pipegen mentions but does not run.
"
`;

exports[`doc: the 18 sections > doc: snapshot > shape B 1`] = `
"# What pipegen set up in this repository

pipegen wrote this file in plain English. If you edit it, pipegen keeps your edits and never replaces the file.

## What your GitHub plan allows

This section comes first so that no limit is hidden.

- This repository is public. Your GitHub plan: free.
- Branch rules: available, so the check \`pipegen / gate\` can be required before a pull request merges.
- Environments: not available on this plan, so there is no \`pipegen-signing\` environment: the verify job runs without one, and the default-branch limit can not be enforced there.
- Required reviewers on an environment: not available, so a deploy does not wait for a person.
- Deployment branch policies: not confirmed, so pipegen does not store a signing key in key mode.
- Build attestations: not available on this plan.
- Code scanning results: available.
- Keyless signing: allowed (a public repository: its signing log is public).
- Anyone who can add a workflow can add a job that reports under the name of the gate. The guard is CODEOWNERS plus a required code-owner review of the pipeline files. On a private repository with a free plan GitHub can not enforce that review, so this is a limit that stays.
- Where GitHub can not enforce this (a private repository on a free plan, or a repository with one writer), the deploy check trusts the people who can edit the workflow files.
- Pull requests from forks: on a public repository, a first-time contributor's workflow run waits for a maintainer to approve it. On a private repository, workflows on fork pull requests are off by default. A check that needs a secret shows as not run on a fork pull request.
- The network block on CI jobs lets only the allowed hosts through. A site that sits behind the same CDN as an allowed host stays reachable, so the block limits traffic and is not a wall.
- On a public repository anyone can submit a review on a pull request, and that starts a run on the review event. It does not cancel the pull request's own run.

## What pipegen set up

- pipegen v0.2 is for repositories owned by CrisWorking. A public repository uses the gate in the normal way.
- This repository is public, so its checks are written inline in this repository: \`.github/workflows/pipegen.yml\`, \`.github/workflows/pipegen-merge.yml\`, \`.github/workflows/pipegen-push.yml\` and \`.github/workflows/pipegen-nightly.yml\`. Its install record is kept in a private pipelines repository.
- pipegen does not change what or how you deploy. It adds a verify job, \`needs\` entries on your existing deploy jobs, and its head check as the first step of each of those jobs (so a deploy that waited for an approval, or a re-run, stops once a newer commit is on the default branch), and nothing else in them. Two kinds of deploy job get no head check: a job that calls another workflow (it has no steps of its own), and one pipegen gates only for its runner or vars that can not start with the check; the notes below say which jobs and what that leaves open.
- pipegen offers three strictness levels, Starter, Standard (the default) and Strict. They decide which checks block a pull request and which only warn.
- Estimated monthly Actions minutes: about 120, counted from the activity of the last 30 days. Actions minutes on a public repository are free.

### Services pipegen found

- Service "docs-site" (node), type: static-site (set by you).

## Fix a blocked pull request

The required check is \`pipegen / gate\`. When it fails, or only warns, its summary on the run gives messages. The first column holds the start of the message as the gate writes it (or the rule name of an integrity finding), so you can find your line.

| Message | What it means | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a new critical, high or secret finding blocks in any mode | A check found a serious problem or a secret that the base branch did not have. | Fix it in the pull request. A false alarm is for a code owner to judge; adding a suppression comment is flagged itself. Treat a secret as leaked and rotate it: a secret that was ever pushed stays in the history. |
| secret added in this PR's history | A scan of the commits of this pull request found a secret. A commit that added a secret still counts after a later commit took it out. Any finding in the commit range blocks. | Rotate the secret now. Then rewrite the branch so the commits no longer hold it, and push again. |
| the pull request's commits are not proven free of secrets | The scan of the commits of this pull request could not run or gave no result, so nothing is proven. It blocks on a pull request. | Run the job again. |
| a new vulnerability in dependency review | A package added by the pull request has a known vulnerability that the base did not have. | Pick another package or version. |
| a malicious package advisory (MAL-): blocks even when old | A dependency matches a malicious-package advisory. It blocks even when it is old. | Remove the package and rotate anything it could have read. |
| an unknown licence: needs a human, never blocks | A package has a licence the check does not know. It only warns. | Have a person check the licence. |
| integrity:protected-path | The pull request changes a workflow, a tool config or an ignore file (a file that is part of the pipeline), and no code owner approved this exact commit. An approval counts only for the commit it was given on, so a code owner who approved an older commit has to approve the latest one. | Ask a code owner to approve the latest commit. After a new push the approval has to be given again. |
| integrity:suppression | Added lines turn a check off for that code (a suppression comment). | Remove the suppression, or ask a code owner to approve this exact commit. |
| integrity:incomplete-list | The pull request is too large to check (3,000 or more files), or GitHub did not give the whole file list, so the gate can not see every change. | Split the pull request into smaller ones, or run the checks again if the branch moved. |
| integrity:no-patch | GitHub gave no readable change for that file, so the gate can not check it for suppressions. | Make the change smaller or split the file change into its own pull request. A code owner can approve the exact commit. |
| integrity:case-extension | A new file has a scanned language's extension in another letter case, so the scanners would skip it while a runtime may still load it. | Rename the file to the lower-case extension. |
| pipeline files changed or suppressions added without code-owner approval on the current commit | This heading of the summary comes with the integrity findings above. The pull request is blocked until a code owner approves the current commit. | Ask a code owner to approve the latest commit. |
| installing pipegen: review these files | This is the pull request that installs pipegen, so the pipeline files are new. It warns and does not block. | Read the files, then merge. |
| tool error in | A check could not finish (it crashed, ran out of time or its result could not be read). A required check that errors blocks; a not proven check only warns. | Read the check's log, fix the cause and run the job again. |
| did not report | A scanner finished without a result. A required one blocks; a not proven or non-blocking one only warns or notes it. | Run the job again. |
| not run on a fork PR (no secrets) | GitHub gives no secrets to a pull request from a fork, so a check that needs one did not run. If the check is required, the pull request is blocked. | A maintainer can push the branch into this repository and open the pull request from there. |
| not-proven check | pipegen could not prove the check on your repository yet (see Not proven yet). It warns and does not block. | Set the secret names listed under Not proven yet. The nightly run then opens an issue and \`npx pipegen update\` applies the change. |
| non-blocking check | A check that never blocks. The summary notes its result. | Read the note; nothing is required. |
| warn-first check | A check judged by its job status, in its own warn period: a failed job is a warning until a reviewed pull request makes the check blocking. | Fix the failure before the period ends (see Warn mode). |
| warn-first: a warning until a reviewed pull request makes the check blocking | A finding of a check that reads findings, in its own warn period, is a warning. A new critical, high or secret finding still blocks, and so does a failed job of such a check (an image scan or a Dockerfile lint, for example). | Fix the findings before the period ends (see Warn mode). |
| not proven: never blocks | A finding of a check that is not proven yet is a warning and never blocks. | Make the check proven (see Not proven yet). |
| a non-blocking check: never blocks | A finding of a check that is not meant to block is shown and never blocks. | Read it; nothing is required. |
| required check | A required check failed, so the pull request is blocked. | Open the job's log, fix the failure and push again. |
| is not in the job list (missing) | The gate expects a check that did not run, so the pull request can not merge. | Run the workflow again. With the org required workflow, re-run the \`pipegen required\` run after a code owner approves. |
| the job list is empty | No check ran, so nothing is proven and the gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| result tampered or missing | The result file of a check is missing, or its checksum differs from the one its job reported. The gate does not trust it and blocks. | Run the checks again. If it keeps happening, someone changed the pipeline: run \`npx pipegen verify\`. |
| no coverage for | A check ran but could not look (for example a scan that reported nothing because it had nothing to read). A required one blocks, because nothing was proven. | Fix what the check could not read, then run the checks again. |
| no base: | The base scan could not be used for this event, so every finding compared with it counts as new. A new critical or high finding therefore blocks, and so does a new secret. Only a scanner that could not run on the base commit of the pull request that installs pipegen warns instead (the next row). | Run the checks again. On a new install, merge the setup pull request and the next pull request has a base. |
| no base: the scanner couldn't run on the base commit (installing pipegen) | Image findings have no earlier scan to compare with yet: on the pull request that installs pipegen they warn, and on every other pull request they block. | Fix the finding in the image or its base image. |
| input is refused | One of the gate's own inputs (the job list, the policy, the service roots, the protected paths, a date) is not one the gate accepts. The gate blocks, because it can not trust its own settings. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| input is missing | One of the gate's own inputs (the job list, the GitHub token, the service roots) is missing. The gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| the jobs input (toJSON(needs)) is | The results of the jobs the gate reads are missing, too large or not an object. The gate blocks. | Run the workflow again. If it keeps happening, run \`npx pipegen verify\`. |
| the mode input is not gate | The gate was started in a mode other than gate. The gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| does not take the input | The gate was given an input it does not know, or the wrong mode. The gate blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| the policy is refused | The gate's own settings are wrong, so it uses the default policy and blocks. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| not-proven can't include floor need | A check every repository needs is listed as not proven, which the gate does not allow. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| can never be skipped | A check that can never be skipped is listed as skipped. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| warn-first can't be set on floor need | A check every repository needs was put in warn-first mode, which the gate does not allow. | Run \`npx pipegen verify\`, then \`npx pipegen update\`. |
| skipped, | You skipped that part with --skip, so it has no job and gives no protection. The summary lists it under Skipped. | Run pipegen again without the skip if you want the part. |
| (warn mode) | The summary heading says warn mode: old findings and medium ones warn, and a new critical, high or secret finding still blocks. | Fix the warnings before warn mode ends (see Warn mode). |
| (block mode) | The summary heading says block mode: the warn period is over, so what used to warn now blocks. | Fix the finding. Nothing else changed. |

## The four layers

Each check has a tool, a reason and a mode. The mode is worked out from what pipegen wrote and from the gate's own rules, not only from what the plan asked for.

### Pre-commit layer

Runs on your machine before each commit.

No check of this layer is set up here.

### Push layer

Runs on a push to a branch that has no open pull request.

No check of this layer is set up here.

### Merge layer

Runs on every pull request and decides whether it can merge. The gate (the check \`pipegen / gate\`) reads every result.

A check marked "Blocks a pull request" blocks only after you run \`npx pipegen finish\`, which turns on the merge locks. Until then a failed gate shows on the pull request but does not stop the merge.

- Secret scan, tool betterleaks/betterleaks. Looks for passwords, keys and tokens in the code and in the commits of a pull request. Blocks a pull request.
- Code scan, service "docs-site", tool semgrep/semgrep. Looks for insecure code patterns in your source. Blocks a pull request.
- Dependency scan, service "docs-site", tool google/osv-scanner. Looks for known vulnerabilities in the packages you depend on. Blocks a pull request.
- The gate, tool the pipegen gate action. Reads every result and decides whether the pull request can merge. Blocks a pull request.
- Workflow lint, tool rhysd/actionlint. Checks GitHub workflow files for unsafe patterns. Blocks a pull request.
- Workflow pin check, tool suzuki-shunsuke/pinact. Checks that the actions in workflows are pinned to a commit. Blocks a pull request.

### Deploy layer

Runs on a push to the default branch: it builds, signs and checks the images that are deployed.

No check of this layer is set up here.

### Limits of these checks

- The code scan runs Semgrep with a pinned copy of the semgrep-rules repository. Those rules have their own licence (the Semgrep Rules License v1.0). It lets you use them to check your own code, but not to sell them or offer them as a service. Read it before you use the rules anywhere else.
- Each service installs with \`npm ci\`, which needs a \`package-lock.json\` in that service's folder. A lock file at the workspace root is not enough: the check then fails closed, it does not pass.
- npm only: pnpm, yarn and bun are not supported yet. A Vue, Svelte or Astro TypeScript repository stops at assembly with a plain reason, because pipegen has no reviewed type checker for it.
- The nightly job has a \`concurrency\` group, so runs never overlap. An issue it opened and any bot closed (even a stale bot running as the github-actions bot) is reopened by design. Only a person can dismiss it.
- After rewriting main's history (a force-push), the gate may stay in block mode until 10 green pushes to main have followed.

### Nightly scans

- Every night a scan reads the whole git history (every branch and tag) for secrets and reports what it finds in code scanning. It never blocks a pull request: a secret it finds has already leaked, so rotate it.
- A permanent limit of the history scan: a merge commit with three or more parents (an octopus merge) anywhere in the history makes the nightly history job fail every night, because its own changes can not be shown. Only a history rewrite removes it.
- Another permanent limit: a commit dated after 1 January 2100 on any branch or tag makes the same job fail every night, because the scan proves it read the whole history with marker commits of that date.

